    "obs_and_metrics": [],
    "innovation": "This paper demonstrates through hindcast experiments that initialized decadal climate prediction models could have successfully simulated the early-2000s global warming hiatus by correctly predicting the associated negative phase of the Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation, a feat missed by traditional, uninitialized climate model averages.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "tropical Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A composite of those ten ensemble members out of 262 possible CMIP5 realizations (Methods) shows a negative phase of the IPO, characterized by cooler-than-normal average surface temperatures over the tropical Pacific, with opposite sign anomalies in the northwest and southwest Pacific, lasting 14 years (Fig. 1b and Supplementary Fig. 1).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "northwest Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A composite of those ten ensemble members out of 262 possible CMIP5 realizations (Methods) shows a negative phase of the IPO, characterized by cooler-than-normal average surface temperatures over the tropical Pacific, with opposite sign anomalies in the northwest and southwest Pacific, lasting 14 years (Fig. 1b and Supplementary Fig. 1).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "southwest Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A composite of those ten ensemble members out of 262 possible CMIP5 realizations (Methods) shows a negative phase of the IPO, characterized by cooler-than-normal average surface temperatures over the tropical Pacific, with opposite sign anomalies in the northwest and southwest Pacific, lasting 14 years (Fig. 1b and Supplementary Fig. 1).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "tropical Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "This is a compelling application of the result derived from other analyses, in that tropical Pacific surface temperatures in the negative phase of the naturally-occurring IPO can temporarily counteract the warming from increasing GHGs to produce a hiatus of warming in globally averaged surface air temperatures that can last for a decade or more2-5, even as the climate system is still trapping excess heat of about 0.5-1.0 W m\u00af\u00b2 (refs 15,16).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "tropical Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The prediction of the transition to the positive phase of the IPO with positive surface temperatures over the tropical Pacific in observations (Supplementary Fig. 2a) is represented by a significant anomaly pattern correlation with the initialized prediction of +0.60 in Fig. 2b.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "tropical Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Here the transition of the surface temperature pattern to the negative phase of the IPO is simulated with cooler than normal temperatures in the tropical Pacific (Fig. 3b), with a significant anomaly pattern correlation with observations of +0.75 (Fig. 2d).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CMIP5 models",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "globally averaged surface air temperature",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Figure 1 | Climate model simulations of the early-2000s hiatus. a, Time series of globally averaged surface air temperature anomalies (\u00b0C) in relation to the 1986-2005 time period for the observations (black lines with 5%, median and 95% values from HadCRUT4), and individual ensemble members from the 262 uninitialized CMIP5 simulations (light grey); red lines are individual ensemble members that simulate the early-2000s hiatus as observed (linear trend less than 0.04 \u00b0C per decade) for the period 2000-2013; the dashed line indicates the transition year (2005) between the CMIP5 historical and scenario experiments.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CMIP5 models",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "warming trend less than 0.04\u00b0C per decade",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "However, inspection of the individual ensemble members from these same model simulations reveals that ten members actually produced the observed warming trend (defined as a trend less than 0.04 \u00b0C per decade as observed) during the period of the hiatus 2000\u20132013 (Fig. 1a and refs 4,13).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CMIP5 models",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "free-running climate simulations",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "Traditional free-running climate simulations that start in the mid-nineteenth century and proceed through the twentieth century with observed human-produced forcings, such as increasing greenhouse gases (GHGs), aerosols and ozone, along with natural forcings, such as aerosols from volcanic eruptions and solar variability, are designed to simulate the response of the climate system to those changes in external forcings.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CMIP5 multi-models",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "initialized decadal climate prediction",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "If the recent methodology of initialized decadal climate prediction could have been applied in the mid-1990s using the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project Phase 5 multi-models, both the negative phase of the IPO in the early 2000s as well as the hiatus could have been simulated, with the multi-model average performing better than most of the individual models.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
